verses.text:
Yahweh is kind,
a refuge when trouble comes;
he cares for those who shelter in him.

But with an overflowing flood
he will make a full end of her place,{c}
and darkness shall pursue his enemies.

What do you devise against Yahweh?
He will make a full end;
distress shall not rise up a second time.

For though they are tangled like thorns
and drunk as with their drink,
they shall be devoured like stubble fully dried.{d}

From you has gone out one
who devises evil against Yahweh,
a counselor of worthlessness.{e}

Behold, upon the mountains the feet of him who brings good news,
who proclaims peace!{f}
Celebrate your feasts, O Judah;
pay your vows;
for the worthless one shall not again pass through you;
he is cut off completely.

One who scatters has come up against your face.{a}
Guard the rampart;
watch the road;
brace your loins;
gather all your strength.

The shield of his mighty men is made red;
the warriors are clothed in scarlet;
the chariots gleam with the fire of steel
on the day he prepares for battle,
and the spears of cypress are brandished.{b}

The chariots rage in the streets;
they rush to and fro in the squares;
their appearance is like torches;
they dart about like the lightning.

He calls to mind his nobles;
they stumble as they march;
they hurry to her wall,
and the siege shield is set up.

It is decided: she is stripped; she is carried off,{c}
and her maidservants moan like the sound of doves,
beating upon their breasts.

“I am your enemy,” announces Yahweh of Armies,{e}
“and I will set your chariots ablaze,{f}
the sword will eat up your young lions,
I will remove your prey from the land,
and the cry of your envoys will never again be heard.”

Woe to the city of blood,
all of it full of lies and plunder;
the prey never departs!

And it shall come to pass that all who look on you shall shrink from you and say,
‘Nineveh is laid waste; who will grieve for her?’
Where shall I seek comforters for you?”{a}

Are you better than No-amon,{b}
that sat by the Nile channels,
with water all around her,
whose rampart was the sea,
and water her wall?{c}

Yet she too went into exile;
she went into captivity;
her little ones also were dashed to pieces
at the head of every street;
they cast lots for her honored men,
and all her great men were bound in chains.

You also shall be drunk;
you shall go into hiding;
you also shall seek a refuge from the enemy.

All your strongholds are fig trees with the firstfruits:
if they are shaken,
they fall into the mouth of the eater.

Behold, your troops in your midst are women.
The gates of your land are thrown wide open to your enemies;
fire has devoured your bars.

Draw water for the siege;
strengthen your fortresses;
go into the clay and tread the mortar;
take hold of the brick mold!

There the fire shall devour you;
the sword shall cut you off;
it shall devour you as the young locust devours.
Multiply yourself like the young locust;
multiply yourself like the locust swarm!{e}

You have multiplied your merchants
more than the stars of the heavens;
the young locust strips and flies away.

Your guardsmen are like the locust swarm,
and your officials like clouds of grasshoppers
that settle in the walls on a cold day;
when the sun rises, they flee,
and no one knows the place where they are.{f}
